#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
Pallet Detector Node
====================
YOLO OBB-based pallet detection using RGB-D camera.
Publishes pallet pose in map / base_footprint / camera frames and dynamic TF.

Subscribes:
  - /color/image_raw (Image)
  - /depth/image_raw (Image)
  - /color/camera_info (CameraInfo; configurable via camera_info_topic)

Publishes:
  - /pallet/pose (geometry_msgs/PoseStamped)        - absolute pose in `map` frame
  - /pallet/pose_base (geometry_msgs/PoseStamped)   - robot-relative pose in `base_footprint`
  - /pallet/pose_camera (geometry_msgs/PoseStamped) - raw pose in camera optical frame
  - /pallet/detections (vision_msgs/Detection2DArray)
  - /perception/pallet_debug_image (sensor_msgs/Image)

Broadcasts:
  - pallet_frame (dynamic TF, parent = map frame)
  - pallet_frame_base (dynamic TF, parent = base_footprint)

Model classes (current weights): box, edge, hang, hole, pallet-hole-segmentation1
  - The class used as "pallet" is the parameter `pallet_class`
    (default 'pallet-hole-segmentation1').
  - The class used as "edge" is the parameter `edge_class` (default 'edge').

Frame conventions
-----------------
- Camera optical frame: X right, Y down, Z forward (what pixel_to_3d returns).
- ROS body frame (REP-103): X forward, Y left, Z up.
  The optical -> ROS conversion is done by TF (camera_color_optical_frame is
  linked to camera_link / base_footprint by the robot's TF tree), so every
  point/orientation is carried through the real TF rotation + translation.
- Camera tilt: the physical camera looks down by `camera_pitch_deg` (default 7)
  but the URDF has camera_link rpy = 0, so the node adds that tilt itself when
  going optical -> base/map. Depth Z is distance ALONG the optical axis, not the
  horizontal distance, so with a tilted camera Base X < depth.
- Pallet frame: origin at pallet centre, +X is the normal of the pallet's long
  (front) face pointing INTO the pallet (away from the robot), +Z up.
  Yaw is the angle of pallet +X relative to base_footprint +X, around the
  vertical axis, normalized to [-pi, pi]:
      yaw = 0      -> robot is facing the pallet squarely
      yaw > 0      -> pallet is rotated counter-clockwise (left) vs robot heading
      yaw < 0      -> pallet is rotated clockwise (right) vs robot heading
  The yaw is estimated by fitting lines (RANSAC + least squares) to the valid
  depth pixels inside (a) the `pallet` OBB (front face) and (b) every `edge` OBB
  that belongs to that pallet (edge label drawn along a pallet edge). By default
  the edge is the SIDE edge, perpendicular to the front face in 3D (in the image it
  looks like a parallelogram, so no right angle is assumed there: everything is
  done in 3D from depth); its direction gives the yaw +-90 deg. Vertical edges
  are rejected automatically (they collapse to a point). The estimates
  are combined with weights (inverse variance, edge trusted more by default) and
  inconsistent ones are dropped. The result is then smoothed over the last frames
  (median + dead-band) so the value stays steady. The position (x, y, z) gets the
  same multi-frame averaging; a real jump (3 frames in a row) restarts the average.
  ROI: only detections whose centre is in the lower part of the image (floor) are
  used (`roi_top_ratio`). If nothing can be fitted, the
  last steady yaw is kept for up to 1 s, otherwise it falls back to the
  line-of-sight direction (pallet assumed perpendicular to the ray robot -> pallet).

POSITION (x, y): by default (`use_face_center`) it is the MIDPOINT of the fitted
  front-face line (centre of the visible face, on the face plane), not the ray through
  the YOLO box centre + a depth percentile. The pixel method is biased sideways and in
  depth when the pallet is rotated (bias grows with the tilt, its sign follows the tilt).
  The midpoint is only used when the visible face width is plausible
  (`face_min_ratio` .. `face_max_ratio` times `pallet_width_m`); otherwise (partial view)
  the node falls back to the pixel method and logs a warning. The HUD shows the source
  (`face` / `pixel`) and the visible face width.
"""

import math
import os
import traceback
import time
import threading
from collections import deque
from typing import Optional, List, Tuple

import cv2
import numpy as np

import rclpy
from rclpy.node import Node
from rclpy.duration import Duration
from rclpy.time import Time
from rclpy.qos import QoSProfile, ReliabilityPolicy

from geometry_msgs.msg import PoseStamped, TransformStamped, Quaternion
from sensor_msgs.msg import Image, CameraInfo
from vision_msgs.msg import Detection2DArray, Detection2D, ObjectHypothesisWithPose
